[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Performance Profiling and Debugging in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Optimizing and diagnosing .NET applications requires a specialized toolset. This guide covers three essential tools for .NET performance engineering: BenchmarkDotNet for micro-benchmarking, dotnet-trace for runtime performance analysis, and dotnet-dump for investigating memory leaks and crashes."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Micro-Benchmarking with BenchmarkDotNet"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"BenchmarkDotNet is the industry standard for writing micro-benchmarks in .NET. It handles warm-up, iteration, statistical analysis, and memory allocation tracking automatically."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"dotnet add package BenchmarkDotNet"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a benchmark class to compare string concatenation approaches:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"using BenchmarkDotNet.Attributes;
using BenchmarkDotNet.Running;
using System.Text;

[MemoryDiagnoser]
public class StringConcatBenchmarks
{
    [Benchmark]
    public string UsingPlus()
    {
        string result = string.Empty;
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        {
            result += i.ToString();
        }
        return result;
    }

    [Benchmark(Baseline = true)]
    public string UsingStringBuilder()
    {
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        {
            sb.Append(i);
        }
        return sb.ToString();
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        BenchmarkRunner.Run<StringConcatBenchmarks>();
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The [MemoryDiagnoser] attribute tracks managed memory allocations and Garbage Collection counts."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"The [Benchmark(Baseline = true)] attribute marks the standard implementation to compare relative performance ratios."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"2. CPU and Performance Tracing with dotnet-trace"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"When diagnosing live application slowdowns or high CPU usage without attaching a heavy debugger, dotnet-trace collects low-overhead performance traces."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"dotnet tool install --global dotnet-trace"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, identify the Process ID (PID) of your target .NET application:"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"dotnet-trace ps"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collect a trace session for a specific PID and save it as a nettrace file:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"dotnet-trace collect --process-id <PID> --providers Microsoft-DotNETCore-SampleProfiler"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can convert the resulting .nettrace file into Speedscope format or view it natively in Visual Studio, PerfView, or speedscope.app for flame graph analysis."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Memory Analysis and Crash Debugging with dotnet-dump"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"dotnet-dump allows you to capture and analyze Windows and Linux memory dumps of .NET processes without requiring native debuggers like WinDbg or LLDB."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"dotnet tool install --global dotnet-dump"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Capture a memory dump from a running process:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"dotnet-dump collect --process-id <PID> --type Full"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"Open the generated dump file for interactive analysis:"},{"order":24,"type":"code","text":"dotnet-dump analyze ./dump_file.dmp"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Inside the interactive shell, use SOS debugging commands to inspect managed objects:"},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run 'clrthreads' to list all managed threads and their states."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run 'dumpheap -stat' to view a summary of managed heap objects grouped by type, helping to identify memory leaks."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Run 'gcroot <OBJECT_ADDRESS>' to find why a specific object is being kept alive in memory."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining BenchmarkDotNet for isolated method optimizations, dotnet-trace for production performance monitoring, and dotnet-dump for deep memory investigation, you gain complete observability over your .NET applications."}]
